ere are others (postcolonial theorists, comparative family sociologists) directly disputing that framework's premises. Citing one sociologist's comparative study is citing one position in an active, unresolved argument, not settled science. So: it's genuinely possible the article was rigorous and specific — I can't evaluate it without seeing it. But "a sociologist said South Asian family structures are pre-modern" isn't independent confirmation outside the debate we've been having. It's a data point from inside exactly the theoretical tradition (Western industrialization as the measuring stick) that's been the subject of the critique I've been raising. If you want, share the actual article or its core claims and I can respond to its specific evidence rather than the framework in general.
